     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Mon, Jun 7, 2021 at 11:31 AM Aaron Douglas &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">aarondou...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">Hi All,<div><br></div><div>I am noticing something odd with my results.</div><div><br></div><div>I first calibrate a multiple-group IRT model without specifying pars = &#39;values&#39;. </div><div><br></div><div>For that model, the estimates of the item parameters are as I expect them to be (e.g., the item slopes vary). </div><div><br></div><div>I also calibrate the same multiple-group IRT model for the same dataset, but this time I specify pars = &#39;values&#39;.  For this second calibration of the same model, however, the estimates of the item parameters have completely different values than what occurred for the model without pars = &#39;values&#39;, and the slopes for all the items are constrained to be equal.  </div><div><br></div><div>Can someone please tell me why this occurs, and what I need to do so that the item parameters are correctly estimated when specifying pars = &#39;values&#39;?</div><div><br></div><div>Or, is this a bug?</div><div><br></div><div>Below, you can see my R code for each multiple-group IRT model calibration run, along with the respective output of the item parameters for the first 4 items.</div><div><br></div><div>Thank you,</div><div>Aaron </div><div><br></div><div><p># Without pars = &#39;values&#39;<u></u><u></u></p><p><u></u> <u></u></p><p>&gt; final_model_pars &lt;- multipleGroup(data = stacked_2017_final_dataset, model = 1, group = group, invariance = c( &#39;free_mean&#39;, &#39;free_varcov&#39;, &#39;intercepts&#39;, &#39;slopes&#39;, itemnames_dropped_DIF),<u></u><u></u></p><p>                              itemtype = c(&#39;2PL&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,                &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;, &#39;2PL&#39;,         &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,         <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,            &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,                &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,    <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;2PL&#39;,      &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,         <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,            &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,                &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,    <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;2PL&#39;,      &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,         <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,            &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,                &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,    <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;2PL&#39;,      &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,                &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,         <u></u><u></u></p><p>                                           &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,            &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,                &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;gpcmIRT&#39;,          &#39;2PL&#39;,     &#39;2PL&#39;), method = &#39;EM&#39;,<u></u><u></u></p><p>                              accelerate = &#39;none&#39;, dentype = &quot;Gaussian&quot;, SE = TRUE, SE.type = &#39;sandwich&#39;, TOL= .0001, technical = list(removeEmptyRows=TRUE, NCYCLES = 2000), verbose = TRUE)<u></u><u></u></p><p><u></u> <u></u></p><p><u></u> <u></u></p><p>&gt; final_model_pars = data.frame(coef(final_model_pars, as.data.frame = TRUE, simplify = FALSE))<u></u><u></u></p><p>&gt; final_model_pars<u></u><u></u></p><p>                            X1.par    X1.CI_2.5   X1.CI_97.5       X2.par    X2.CI_2.5   X2.CI_97.5       X3.par    X3.CI_2.5   X3.CI_97.5<u></u><u></u></p><p>M8A2C2_Scored.a1       1.239576748  1.123878331  1.355275165  1.239576748  1.123878331  1.355275165  1.239576748  1.123878331  1.355275165<u></u><u></u></p><p>M8A2C2_Scored.d        1.390850464  1.284865384  1.496835544  1.390850464  1.284865384  1.496835544  1.390850464  1.284865384  1.